00:51:53.820 --> 00:51:56.250
Okay, so we introduced a
practical framework for

00:51:56.250 --> 00:51:59.400
simulating couple bubble sounds,
it involves a coupling model

00:51:59.430 --> 00:52:02.160
based on the mass matrix and an
efficient way to compute

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:06.150
airborne sound on the GPU. Just
to give you an idea, so the

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:11.430
basic model of a bubble is just
an independent oscillator. So in

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:15.510
a pressure volume frame, we have
forcing due to pressure and then

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:19.560
the volume fluctuations of this
bubble are modeled, here's this

00:52:19.560 --> 00:52:22.770
v. And then so this is a simple
harmonic oscillator, if you have

00:52:22.770 --> 00:52:25.500
a bunch of bubbles, they're all
the couple simple harmonic

00:52:25.500 --> 00:52:29.790
oscillators. And once you
introduce coupling, the pressure

00:52:29.790 --> 00:52:33.420
from one bubble can can drive
the other bubbles essentially.

00:52:33.420 --> 00:52:38.670
So as you know, it changes its
volume, there's some, you know,

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:42.840
incompressible, velocity field
that causes pressure changes

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:46.410
elsewhere. And so this
introduces coupling, and if you

00:52:46.590 --> 00:52:50.010
symmetrize the system, you end
up with a mass matrix

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:53.850
formulation, something like
this. So you basically have off

00:52:53.850 --> 00:52:56.880
diagonal entries in your mass
matrix coupling these bubbles

00:52:56.880 --> 00:52:59.880
together. So this is a weird
system, usually where you have

00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:03.780
like, you know, you're used to a
sparse stiffness matrix. But

00:53:03.780 --> 00:53:06.540
this is basically just due to
the gas and surface tension. So

00:53:06.540 --> 00:53:09.750
it's diagonal, and you have all
the off diagonal coupling in the

00:53:09.750 --> 00:53:12.480
mass matrix, which is a bit
awkward, because if you want to

00:53:12.480 --> 00:53:17.940
timestep, this, now you have a
dense matrix. So the coupling is

00:53:17.940 --> 00:53:21.300
smaller if they're farther away,
but bubbles come and go. So you

00:53:21.300 --> 00:53:23.970
add a new bubble, you have to
increase the mass matrix size,

00:53:23.970 --> 00:53:27.300
if you did pops or something,
you have to decrease it. So this

00:53:27.300 --> 00:53:31.980
is introduces changes in this.
And then you have this problem

00:53:31.980 --> 00:53:35.010
that you have the time step at
really high rates. So this is an

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:38.220
awful space to be in. Like even
if you want to do a, you know,

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:41.880
forward Euler time step or some,
you know, midpoint, you'd still

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:45.180
have like a dense matrix, you
have to solve to do an explicit

00:53:45.180 --> 00:53:49.200
salt method. So this is this is
awful and tiny time steps. So

00:53:49.230 --> 00:53:52.740
the way to deal with this, we
factorize the mass matrix

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:55.980
Szalinski. And then it's
positive definite, we can

00:53:56.010 --> 00:53:58.320
interpolate the inverses. And
they'll still be positive. So

00:53:58.320 --> 00:54:02.490
this is good. And so this allows
us to interpolate these

00:54:02.490 --> 00:54:07.410
factorizations along the way, to
make something go fast, the

00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:10.500
matrix can still lose positive
definiteness as bubbles get

00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:15.660
closer, it's not guaranteed. So
we regularize the Symmetrize

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:19.380
mass matrix, and this, we can
show this fixes it. And then you

00:54:19.380 --> 00:54:24.360
can also do low rank update
